# Chroma content pack, Stage and Screen: Acting, Theatre, Film and Television (modern Earth first, with the tribal and
# magic worlds). PART 1 of the pack's moments: DOORS AND HOOKS (moments 1 to 10 of MOMENTS-BRIEF.md): three everyday door
# pairs whose options take a first title by the act (youth theatre member, amateur actor, background artist, drama
# school student, professional actor), and four echoes that open the pathway from a life's own history (the school play,
# retirement, an idea or the wish to be seen, a lucky letter).
# Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: chroma-library/library-spec.md and stage/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md (pack
# rules, calibration lessons, part 1). Names in title:, grants:, requires:, holds: and aims: come from stage/catalogue.py,
# core/reach.py and the base catalogue; world lines follow stage/worlds.py. Everyday pairs split the calls 2 and 3 and
# share stages, ages, holds:, share, share_group, rate and gap; echoes are even on their own (five one-color options and
# one cross cycle), with their engine conditions in stage/engine-conditions.py (ECHO_STAGE).
# Parts carry the real chance of an ordinary person who tries (the brief's pack rules), the same for every color inside a
# moment; the earned-odds lifts (helps.py) come on top in the engine. The lead on every road (Emren's ask): the school
# lead here is [a lead role to remember]; the letter can bring the professional lead; the other doors lead on to
# 'casting night at the local players', 'the summer show casts its lead', 'a screen test', 'the showcase for agents',
# 'opening night at the fringe' and the stage road. Children appear only in safe, supervised forms: a parent or carer
# signs every form that joins, and rehearsals are in groups, in school or with the youth theatre. No real person,
# production, theatre, studio, film, award, agency or union is named.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (checklist P14, value tags): 9 options in this file retagged so the v: values read one of the
# option's own colors (check.py section 4): White's by-the-book singles lead with conformity (not conformity with
# achievement, which reads Blue), Red's singles on impulse lead with stimulation or hedonism (not self-direction,
# which reads Black), Green's singles of home and kin with tradition and benevolence or universalism (not security,
# which reads White), and Red's kindness on the spot with benevolence and stimulation; plus the largest
# disagreements check.py listed (an old custom asked: tradition and conformity; family first: benevolence and
# tradition; kin led away from the chief: self-direction and tradition; a day's pay weighed: power).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair A, open to any child or teenager (no holds): calls WG (cycle s1) / calls UBR (cycle s1)

== a flyer for the youth theatre
tier: everyday
share: .45
share_group: youth_doors
rate: 0.03
times: per child or teenager a year: about 1 in 5 children see an invitation to a youth theatre or a stage school class; the youth theatres' national census counted over 100,000 young people in 414 youth theatres in England in 2024, and about 8 lives in 100 belong to one at some point (catalogue share; estimate)
gap: 2-4
stages: child juvenile
age: 8-17
calls: WG
tone: joy
life: play, school, performing
horizon: week
roles: friend, a parent or carer, a cousin, the youth theatre's leader
earth: a flyer in the school bag for the youth theatre on Saturday mornings
tribal: the old teller calls the children to the fire to learn the small spirits' parts for midwinter
magic: the players' guild's children's company calls for new children at the spring fair, a parent's mark on the roll
scene: A flyer comes home crumpled at the bottom of {Ns} school bag: the youth theatre meets on Saturday mornings in the community hall, with games, a summer show and the first term free. A parent or carer has to sign the form.
scene W: The flyer asks for a signed form and a promise to come every week. {N} likes knowing exactly what is expected, and keeping to it.
scene U: {N} reads the flyer twice: voice games, how a stage fight is made safe, how actors remember all those lines. {N} would like to know how it is done.
scene B: An older girl from the youth theatre was on television last year, and the whole school knows it. {N} looks at the flyer and thinks about that.
scene R: {N} is already acting out a dragon in the kitchen with the flyer in one hand. Saturday cannot come fast enough.
scene G: {friend} goes to the youth theatre already, and so did a cousin before that. It meets in the hall at the end of the road, where the summer fair is held every year.
scene tribal: At dusk the old teller calls the children of the band to the fire. Midwinter is coming, the small spirits need players, and any child whose mother gives leave may come and learn, with the women of the band sitting close by.
scene magic: At the spring fair the players' guild sets up a stall with masks and painted wings. Its children's company wants new children, if a parent will put a mark on the roll, and the guild's matron keeps the little ones together.
- W | ask a parent to sign the form tonight, and be there every Saturday at nine | v: conformity, security | title: youth theatre member | habit | tribal: ask your mother's leave, and sit at the teller's fire every evening until midwinter | magic: ask a parent to put their mark on the roll, and never miss a practice | chance: 85
- U | ask a parent to ring up and find out what the children actually learn there | door | v: self-direction | tribal: ask the old teller what the children learn at the fire before midwinter | magic: ask a parent to find out what the guild's children are taught | chance: 85
- B | get a parent to sign, and ask to go straight into the older children's group | v: achievement, power | title: youth theatre member | tribal: get your mother's leave, and ask the teller for a place among the older children | magic: get a parent's mark on the roll, and ask to start with the older children | chance: 60
- R | put on a show in the kitchen until a parent laughs and signs the form | v: stimulation, hedonism | title: youth theatre member | tribal: dance the hare by the hearth until your mother laughs and gives her leave | magic: play the fairy queen for the family at supper until a parent signs the roll | chance: 85
- G | stay with football on Saturdays, and the team you have played with for years | v: tradition, benevolence | habit | tribal: stay with the children who go to the river with the women, as you always have | magic: stay with the Saturday games on the green, and the friends you have always had | chance: 92
- W>U | ask the class teacher whether it would help with reading aloud in class | v: conformity, self-direction | door | tribal: ask the elder who teaches the children whether the tellings would help with learning the old words | magic: ask your tutor whether the guild's lessons would help with your recitations | chance: 70
- U>B | read the small print, and ask a parent to apply for one of the free places | v: achievement, security | aims: youth theatre member | tribal: find out which children the teller takes without a gift, and ask your mother to speak to the teller | magic: read the guild's notice to the end, and ask a parent to apply for a free place | chance: 70
- B>R | say no: Saturday mornings are for lie-ins and cartoons | v: hedonism, self-direction | habit | tribal: say no: the evenings are for games by the river, not learning parts | magic: say no: feast-day mornings are for sleeping late and the puppet show | chance: 95
- R>G | say you will go only if your best friend from the street comes too | v: benevolence, security | binds | tribal: say you will go to the teller's fire only if your hearth-friend comes too | magic: say you will join only if your best friend from the lane joins too | chance: 70
- G>W | get a parent to sign, then go with your cousin and help with the little ones | v: benevolence, tradition | title: youth theatre member | tribal: go to the teller's fire with your cousin, your mother's leave given, and mind the little ones | magic: go with your cousin, a parent's mark on the roll, and help with the smallest children | chance: 63
works: {N} is there from the very first meeting, and comes home hoarse and happy, full of games and new names.
works: The question is settled within the week, and {N} is glad of the way it went.
fails: Nobody gives the leave in time, and the places are all taken before {N} can have one.
fails: The first meeting is all strangers and loud games, and {N} does not go back.

== auditions for the school production
tier: everyday
share: .45
share_group: youth_doors
rate: 0.03
times: per child or teenager a year: about 1 in 5 meet auditions for a school production they could try for; most secondary schools put on a show a year, with one or two leads (estimate)
gap: 2-4
stages: child juvenile
age: 8-17
calls: UBR
tone: mixed
life: school, performing
horizon: week
roles: friend, rival, the drama teacher, a parent or carer
earth: the auditions notice for the school's spring show on the hall door
tribal: the elders choose which children will play the animals in the great telling at midsummer
magic: the schoolmaster wants players for the feast-day masque, and the best part is the fairy queen
scene: A notice goes up on the hall door: auditions for the spring show on Thursday after school, a speech or a song, everyone welcome. There are two big parts, a dozen small ones, and a crew wanted for the set and the lights.
scene W: The notice lists what to bring and when to arrive, and the drama teacher says everyone who comes prepared will get a part. {N} copies the times into a planner.
scene U: {N} borrows the script and reads the whole play in one evening. The lead has the best lines, and also the hardest ones.
scene B: {rival} is already telling the class the lead is as good as theirs. {N} would very much like to see that face when the cast list goes up.
scene R: {Ns} heart jumps at the notice. A real stage, real lights, the whole school watching: {N} wants it so badly it hurts.
scene G: {Ns} cousin was in the spring show years ago, and the photograph is still on the corridor wall. Half of {Ns} friends are going along on Thursday.
scene tribal: The elders sit at the edge of the dancing ground and call the children one by one: who will be the hare, who the crow, who the great elk in the midsummer telling. The mothers watch from the shade.
scene magic: The schoolmaster pins a notice to the schoolroom door: players wanted for the feast-day masque, and the best part, the fairy queen, still to be cast. The trials are after lessons, with the parents welcome to watch.
- W | offer to cover the lead in case anyone falls ill, and learn every line anyway | v: conformity, benevolence | grants: learning lines | tribal: offer to learn the hare's part too, in case the chosen child falls sick | magic: offer to learn the fairy queen's lines as a second, in case she falls ill | chance: 35
- U | read the whole play first, then audition for the lead with the speech you understand best | v: achievement, self-direction | grants: a lead role to remember | mark: learned a skill | tribal: listen to every old telling of the hare first, then ask the elders for the hare's part | magic: read the whole masque first, then try for the fairy queen with the speech you know best | chance: 23
- B | go for the lead, and let the drama teacher know nobody wants it more | v: achievement, power | grants: a lead role to remember | identity | tribal: ask for the great elk's part, and let the elders see how much you want it | magic: try for the fairy queen, and make sure the schoolmaster knows nobody wants it more | chance: 22
- R | audition for the lead on the spot, with a speech you made up that morning | v: stimulation, self-direction | grants: a lead role to remember | tribal: jump up and play the hare for the elders, making it up as you go | magic: try for the fairy queen with a speech you made up on the way to school | chance: 24
- G | paint the set and run the lights with the friends who never act | v: benevolence, tradition | grants: stagecraft | tribal: help the mothers paint the hide screens and gather wood for the fire | magic: help paint the masque's scenery with the friends who never take a part | chance: 88
- W>U | audition for any part, after asking the drama teacher exactly what it needs | v: conformity, achievement | grants: acting | tribal: ask the elders what each small part needs, and play whichever part they choose | magic: ask the schoolmaster what each part needs, and try for whichever suits | chance: 80
- U>B | join the drama teacher's Saturday youth theatre, where she finds her casts, once a parent signs | v: achievement | title: youth theatre member | tribal: ask your mother's leave to learn at the old teller's fire, where the elders find their players | magic: join the guild's children's company, a parent's mark on the roll, where the masque finds its players | chance: 90
- B>R | skip it: the rehearsals would eat every free evening until the spring holidays | v: hedonism, self-direction | tribal: skip it: the telling would take every evening you could spend at the river | magic: skip it: the practices would eat every evening until the feast | chance: 92
- R>G | audition as a pair with your best friend, happy with any parts going | v: benevolence, stimulation | grants: acting | mark: made a friend | tribal: go before the elders with your hearth-friend, and play two crows together | magic: try out as a pair with your best friend, glad of any parts | chance: 88
- G>W | ask the drama teacher to let the younger ones from your street sing in the chorus | v: benevolence, universalism | door | tribal: ask the elders to let the smallest children of your hearth be the young hares | magic: ask the schoolmaster to let the little ones from your lane sing in the chorus | chance: 30
works: When the parts are given out, {Ns} name is where {N} hoped it would be.
works: Within two weeks {N} knows exactly where to stand, what to do, and whose line is the cue.
fails: When the parts are given out, {Ns} name is further down than {N} hoped.
fails: On the day {N} loses nerve at the door, and walks home instead.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair B, open to teenagers and adults (no holds): calls BR (cycle s2) / calls WUG (cycle s2)

== the local players need a cast
tier: everyday
share: .15
share_group: local_doors
rate: 0.025
times: per teenager or adult a year: about 1 in 25 see a call from the local players they could answer; the UK amateur theatre association's survey counted 437,800 people taking part (2002), and about 4 lives in 100 act with amateurs at some point (catalogue share; estimate)
gap: 3-8
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-90
calls: BR
tone: joy
life: community, leisure, performing
horizon: months
roles: friend, neighbour, colleague, the society's director
earth: a notice in the library window: the local players hold open auditions for the autumn play
tribal: the band's tellers want more players for the midwinter telling
magic: the guild's mystery players need new players for the feast-day pageant
scene: A notice in the library window: the local players hold open auditions for their autumn play on Tuesday evening in the church hall. No experience needed, all ages welcome, and parts for eleven.
scene W: The society has put on two plays a year for forty years, and the notice says so with some pride. {N} likes the thought of helping something that old keep going.
scene U: {N} looks the play up: a comedy of manners in three acts, with one part that is all timing and another that is all stillness. It would be interesting to find out which {N} could do.
scene B: The local paper reviews the players every autumn, and half the town comes to see them. A good part would put {Ns} name about, and the society's chair knows everybody.
scene R: {N} has not been on a stage since school, and has missed it every single year. The thought of the lights and the first-night nerves sets {Ns} heart racing.
scene G: {friend} has acted with the players for years and keeps saying {N} should come. It is the same church hall where {N} went to playgroup, and the same faces at every show.
scene tribal: As the nights lengthen, the band's tellers go from hearth to hearth asking who will play in the midwinter telling. They want hunters for the herd, a voice for the wolf, and someone who can make the children laugh.
scene magic: A notice goes up at the guild hall: the mystery players need new players for the feast-day pageant, no guild token needed, all welcome to try. The pageant wagons are already being painted in the yard.
- W | go to the open auditions, and promise to be at every single rehearsal | v: conformity, benevolence | title: amateur actor | binds | tribal: go to the tellers, and promise to be at the fire every night until midwinter | magic: go to the trials, and swear to every practice until the feast | chance: 75
- U | read the play from the library first, then audition for the part that suits you | v: self-direction, achievement | title: amateur actor | tribal: listen to the old midwinter tellings first, then ask for the part you could play best | magic: read the old pageant first, then try for the part that suits you best | chance: 55
- B | audition, and make sure the director knows you want a part with real lines | v: achievement, power | title: amateur actor | tribal: go to the tellers, and make sure they know you want more than a place in the herd | magic: try out, and let the pageant master know you want a part with lines | chance: 55
- R | turn up on the night and read for anything, just for the fun of it | v: stimulation, hedonism | title: amateur actor | tribal: walk into the tellers' circle and play whatever they ask, for the joy of it | magic: turn up at the trials and read for anything, just for the fun of it | chance: 80
- G | audition alongside the neighbour who has been in every play for twenty years | v: tradition, benevolence | title: amateur actor | habit | tribal: go to the tellers with the old hunter who has played the bear every midwinter of your life | magic: go to the trials with the neighbour who has played in every pageant for twenty years | chance: 65
- W>B | join the society's committee first, and audition once you know how parts are given | v: conformity, power | aims: amateur actor | tribal: sit with the tellers first, and learn how they choose who plays what | magic: join the guild's pageant committee first, and learn how the parts are given | chance: 70
- U>R | watch the dress rehearsal of their spring show, and see whether the spark catches | v: self-direction, stimulation | door | tribal: watch the next telling from the edge of the firelight, and see whether the spark catches | magic: watch a pageant practice from the yard, and see whether the spark catches | chance: 90
- B>G | say no: the autumn evenings belong to the family | v: security, benevolence | mark: turned down a chance | tribal: say no: the winter evenings belong to your own hearth | magic: say no: the evenings belong to the family workshop | chance: 92
- R>W | sell the tickets door to door, with all the cheek you have | v: benevolence, stimulation | body: light | tribal: go to every hearth and call the families to the telling, with all the cheek you have | magic: cry the pageant through the lanes, with all the cheek you have | chance: 65
- G>U | help the old set-builder in his shed, and learn how the flats are made | v: tradition, self-direction | grants: stagecraft | mark: learned a skill | tribal: help the old mask-maker at her fire, and learn how the masks and screens are made | magic: help the old wagon-wright, and learn how the pageant's scenery is built | chance: 80
works: The parts are given out, and {Ns} part has lines worth learning.
works: By the first rehearsal {N} knows half the company by name, and they know {N}.
fails: The night of the choosing clashes with other work, and the parts are given out without {N}.
fails: {N} goes along, reads badly from nerves, and is thanked politely and not called back.

== extras wanted for a film in town
tier: everyday
share: .15
share_group: local_doors
rate: 0.025
times: per teenager or adult a year: about 1 in 25 hear of a call for extras they could answer, more in towns where films are made; the largest US background agency has about 200,000 people registered, and about 8 lives in 1,000 work as an extra at some point (catalogue share; estimate)
gap: 3-8
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-90
calls: WUG
tone: joy
life: work, community, leisure
horizon: week
roles: neighbour, friend, colleague, an assistant director
earth: a film is shooting in the high street, and the background agency wants locals for a day
tribal: a neighbouring band's great telling needs many bodies for the herd, the war band and the dead
magic: the illusionists' procession needs a crowd of townsfolk for its opening night, paid by the night
scene: Trucks, cables and a catering van have taken over the high street, and the shop signs have been changed to a town that does not exist. A notice in the café window says the background agency wants locals for a day: a five o'clock call, a day's pay, and no lines.
scene W: The notice is very clear: arrive at five, bring two changes of plain clothes with no logos, and do exactly what the assistant director says. {N} could do that.
scene U: {N} has always wondered how a film is really made: who decides where the camera goes, and why it takes all day to shoot a minute. Here is a chance to stand in the middle of it.
scene B: A day's pay for standing in a street, and the agency keeps the names of people who turn up on time. {N} wonders how many more days a year that could mean.
scene R: A real film, with real actors, on {Ns} own high street! {N} wants to be in it, even if only as the back of a head.
scene G: The whole street is out watching: the baker, the old men from the bench, the children on their way to school. {N} has known these pavements all {Ns} life, and now they are going to be in a film.
scene tribal: Runners come from the band over the ridge: their great telling needs many bodies for the herd, the war band and the dead, and anyone who walks over will be fed for three days.
scene magic: The illusionists have drawn their wagons up outside the walls, and a crier calls for townsfolk to walk in the opening night's procession, a copper a night, while the glamours blaze overhead.
- W | sign up, be there at five, and stand on the crew's exact mark all day | v: conformity, security | title: background artist | tribal: walk over the ridge, and stand in the herd exactly on the shaper's mark | magic: sign up for the procession, and keep exactly to the marks the illusionist's man gives | chance: 40
- U | sign up, and spend the day watching how the crew sets up every shot | v: self-direction | title: background artist | tribal: walk over the ridge, and watch how their shaper makes the great telling | magic: walk in the procession, and watch how the glamours are cast and held | chance: 35
- B | sign up, and ask what pays extra: your own car, a costume, a line | v: achievement, security | title: background artist | tribal: walk over, and ask what the band gives for words to speak instead of a place in the herd | magic: sign up, and ask what pays more than a copper: a costume, a torch, a line | chance: 15
- R | sign up just to be in a film, and tell everyone you know | v: stimulation, hedonism | title: background artist | tribal: run over the ridge to be in the great telling, and tell every hearth on the way | magic: sign up for the procession for the fun of it, and tell the whole lane | chance: 50
- G | sign up with three neighbours, and stand together in the crowd of your own high street | v: tradition, benevolence | title: background artist | tribal: walk over with your own kin, and play the herd side by side | magic: walk in the procession through your own streets, beside your own neighbours | chance: 15
- W>B | sign with the background agency properly, photographs and all, for the next calls | v: security, achievement | grants: casting directory listing | tribal: ask their shaper to remember your name for the tellings to come | magic: put your name on the illusionists' roll for the processions to come | chance: 70
- U>R | ask an assistant director whether locals are ever given a line | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: ask their shaper whether a player from another band is ever given words to speak | magic: ask the illusionist's man whether townsfolk are ever given a line | chance: 75
- B>G | say no: a day's pay will not cover the day off work | v: power | self_control: + | tribal: say no: three days over the ridge is three days of hunting lost | magic: say no: a copper a night will not cover the shop shut | chance: 92
- R>W | knock on the neighbours' doors so the whole street signs up together | v: benevolence, stimulation | mark: made a friend | tribal: run to every hearth so the whole band walks over together | magic: rouse the whole lane to walk in the procession together | chance: 60
- G>U | watch from the pavement with the neighbours, and see how a film is made | v: tradition, self-direction | door | tribal: watch the great telling from the hillside with your own kin | magic: watch the procession from your doorstep, and see how the glamours are made | chance: 92
works: The day is long and cold and strange, and {N} goes home paid, with a story to tell for years.
works: Whatever {N} chose, it turns out as {N} wanted, and everyone talks of nothing else for a week.
fails: The whole thing is called off at dawn, and {N} hears about it only on arrival.
fails: {N} spends the whole day waiting in the cold, and is used for one moment nobody will ever see.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair C, people who already act (holds: youth theatre member | amateur actor | a lead role to remember | acting): calls WB (cycle s3) / calls URG (cycle s3)
# The places are won at their real chance for an ordinary applicant (a drama school place 10 to 15, paid work from an
# open call 5 to 10), one try in every color; the other acts build the skills, listings and plans that lift those odds.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (checklist P9): the open call's five tries for [professional actor] are long shots (4 or 5, a bold
# try at the next rung by an amateur, an extra or a drama student of two years) and carry `grants_if_fails: a long shot
# that missed`; Black's and Red's go from 6 to 5, and their cross options B>W and R>U from 83 to 84, so every means
# color keeps the same total across the pair.

== drama school auditions
tier: everyday
share: .12
share_group: training_doors
rate: 0.05
times: per young person who acts a year: about 1 in 7 think of applying; about 12,000 apply for about 1,550 places a year at 22 accredited UK drama schools, about 1 in 8 gets one, and about 3 lives in 1,000 train at drama school (catalogue share)
gap: 1-3
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 16-40
calls: WB
holds: youth theatre member | amateur actor | a lead role to remember | acting
tone: mixed
life: education, performing, money
horizon: months
roles: mentor, friend, parent, rival
earth: the drama schools' audition season: forms, fees, two speeches and a song
tribal: an old teller of another band will take one apprentice this winter, and asks to see what the young ones can do
magic: the players' guild school opens its doors for the trials at the spring fair
scene: The drama schools' audition season opens in the autumn: a form and a fee for each school, two contrasting speeches, sixteen bars of a song, and a train to a city for a day of workshops and recalls. Most schools take one applicant in eight, or fewer.
scene W: Each school publishes its rules: the length of the speeches, what to wear, the deadlines. {N} keeps a folder with every one of them, and {mentor} has offered to hear the speeches every week.
scene U: {N} has read that the panels look for truth before polish, and has been trying to work out what that means in a speech written four hundred years ago.
scene B: A place at one of the big schools means agents at the showcase, and agents mean work. {N} counts the fees, the schools and the odds, and wonders which panel matters most.
scene R: {N} wants this more than anything, and cannot sit still for thinking about it. Two speeches, a song, a room of strangers: three minutes to show them everything.
scene G: Nobody in {Ns} family has ever done anything like this, and the nearest school is a long way from home. {N} thinks about the local players, the friends there, and what leaving would mean.
scene tribal: Word comes that an old teller of a band two valleys away will take one apprentice for the winter. The young ones who want it must walk there and play before her fire, and she will choose one.
scene magic: The players' guild school posts its trials at the spring fair: two pieces, one old and one new, a song, and a fee to the guild. The masters take a handful each year from the crowd at the door.
- W | apply to the three schools your drama teacher recommends, and rehearse the speeches by the book | v: conformity | title: drama school student | tribal: walk to the old teller with your own elders' blessing, and play the telling exactly as you were taught | magic: apply to the guild school as your old teacher advises, and learn both pieces by the book | chance: 13
- U | spend months on two speeches you understand line by line, then apply | v: achievement, self-direction | title: drama school student | mark: learned a skill | habit | tribal: spend the autumn on one telling until you know why every word is there, then walk over | magic: spend months on two pieces until you understand every line, then try | chance: 14
- B | apply to every accredited school at once, fees and all, to raise your odds | v: achievement, power | title: drama school student | requires: savings | without: means | lacking: .5 | tribal: walk to every teller who might take an apprentice, with a gift for each | magic: try for every school of players in the realm, and pay every fee | chance: 15
- R | audition with the speech you cannot say without crying, and let the panel see it | v: stimulation, self-direction | title: drama school student | identity | tribal: play the telling that brings the tears, and let the old teller see it | magic: try with the piece that brings the tears, and let the masters see it | chance: 13
- G | audition only at the school in your own city, near family and the local players | v: tradition, benevolence | title: drama school student | tribal: walk only to the teller of the next band along, so your kin are near | magic: try only for the guild school in your own city, near family and friends | chance: 13
- W>R | ask your old drama teacher to hear your speeches every week until the auditions | v: conformity, achievement | habit | tribal: ask the old teller of your own band to hear your telling every night until you go | magic: ask your old teacher to hear your pieces every week until the trials | chance: 85
- U>G | take a foundation year first, and grow into the speeches before auditioning | v: self-direction, security | aims: drama school student | tribal: spend a winter at your own band's fire first, and grow into the telling before walking over | magic: take a year in the guild's preparatory class first, and grow into the pieces | chance: 84
- B>W | take a paid weekend course with one school's own tutors, so they know your face | v: achievement, conformity | door | tribal: bring the old teller a gift in autumn, so she knows your face before the trial | magic: pay for a week of lessons with the guild school's own masters, so they know your face | chance: 83
